To address the issue you're experiencing with your todo list implementation using MooTools, it seems there are a few areas to handle regarding cookie management and reading values properly. Here's a step-wise guide to resolve your problems:

### 1. Counting and Reading Cookies

In JavaScript, cookies are stored in a single string in `document.cookie`. To count how many cookies you've set, you can split this string based on the delimiter `;` which separates each cookie. Here’s how you can do it:

```javascript
function countCookies() {
    return document.cookie.split(';').filter(function(cookie) {
        return cookie.trim() !== '';
    }).length;
}

console.log("Number of cookies set: " + countCookies());
```

### 2. Storing Todos in One Cookie

Instead of writing a separate cookie for each todo item, a better approach is to store all your todo items in a single cookie as a JSON array. This way, you can easily manage and retrieve them. Here's how to modify your code:


### Modified Code

```javascript
window.addEvent('domready', function(){

    $('add').addEvent('click', function(){
        var value = $('todo').value;

        // Create a new Todo item
        var todo = new Todo(value, "beschrijving", new Date(), 1);
        var storeData = JSON.encode(todo);

        // Retrieve existing todos from the cookie
        var existingTodos = Cookie.read('todoList');
        existingTodos = existingTodos ? JSON.decode(existingTodos) : [];

        // Add the new todo to the existing todos
        existingTodos.push(storeData);

        // Store updated todos back to the cookie
        Cookie.write('todoList', JSON.encode(existingTodos), { duration: 1 });

        // Debug: So we can see all todos stored
        console.log(Cookie.read('todoList'));
    });

});

// Your Todo class remains unchanged
var Todo = new Class({
    initialize: function(title, description, date, isDone) {
        this.title = title;
        this.description = description;
        this.date = new Date();
        this.isDone = isDone;
    },

    getTitle: function() {       
        return this.title;  
    },

    getIsDone: function() {
        return this.isDone;
    },

    setIsDone: function(value) {
        this.isDone = value;
    },
});
```

### Explanation:
1. **Single Cookie for Todos:** The todos are now stored in a single cookie called `todoList`. This cookie contains all of your todo items as a JSON array.

2. **Decoding JSON:** When you read the existing cookies, you decode them into an array, add the new todo, and then encode the array back again before writing it to the cookie.

3. **Loop Through Todos:** If you want to loop through the todos, you can simply decode the cookie and access the items:

```javascript
var todos = JSON.decode(Cookie.read('todoList'));
todos.forEach(function(todo) {
    console.log(todo); // This will log each todo item
});
```

### Conclusion
With the above changes, you should be able to effectively manage your todos and retrieve them as needed. This implementation keeps everything neat and manageable in a single cookie, making it easier to handle in your todo application using MooTools!

